{"causes":["J.R. Tolkien drew inspiration from Norse and Celtic mythologies during his academic career.","Tolkien's experiences in World War I deeply influenced his depiction of war and trauma in The Silmarillion.","The rise of interest in mythology and folklore in the early 20th century created a fertile ground for works like The Silmarillion."],"effects":["The Silmarillion has inspired countless authors, artists, and creators across various media platforms.","Tolkien's use of invented languages has had a lasting impact on the field of linguistics.","The book's exploration of themes such as good vs. evil and redemption continues to resonate with readers worldwide."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"j.r.tolkien","sourceName":"J.R. Tolkien","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-silmarillion","targetName":"The Silmarillion","context":"Tolkien drew inspiration from Norse and Celtic mythologies during his academic career."},{"sourceSlug":"amazon-studios","sourceName":"Amazon Studios","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-silmarillion","targetName":"The Silmarillion","context":"In 2019, Amazon Studios adapted The Silmarillion into an animated film."},{"sourceSlug":"norse-mythology","sourceName":"Norse Mythology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-silmarillion","targetName":"The Silmarillion","context":"Tolkien drew heavily from Norse mythology in creating The Silmarillion's narrative."},{"sourceSlug":"celtic-mythology","sourceName":"Celtic Mythology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-silmarillion","targetName":"The Silmarillion","context":"Tolkien also drew inspiration from Celtic mythology during his academic career."},{"sourceSlug":"christian-theology","sourceName":"Christian Theology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-silmarillion","targetName":"The Silmarillion","context":"Tolkien's exploration of Christian theology had a significant impact on The Silmarillion's narrative."},{"sourceSlug":"amazon-studios","sourceName":"Amazon Studios","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-silmarillion","targetName":"The Silmarillion","context":"Amazon Studios promoted the 2019 animated film adaptation of The Silmarillion."},{"sourceSlug":"book-exhibitions","sourceName":"Book Exhibitions","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-silmarillion","targetName":"The Silmarillion","context":"The book has been featured in various exhibitions celebrating J.R. Tolkien's works."},{"sourceSlug":"j.r.tolkien","sourceName":"J.R. Tolkien","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"the-silmarillion","targetName":"The Silmarillion","context":"Tolkien wrote The Silmarillion in the early 20th century."},{"sourceSlug":"amazon-studios","sourceName":"Amazon Studios","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-silmarillion","targetName":"The Silmarillion","context":"The book's renewed popularity has sparked interest in its narrative and characters."}],"places":[{"name":"Birmingham, England","role":"Academic Career"},{"name":"Oxford, England","role":"University of Oxford"},{"name":"London, England","role":"Publishing Industry"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Silmarillion","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-silmarillion"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q79762","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Silmarillion"],"tags":["literary work","Epic Poetry & Mythology","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"'In the beginning Eru, the One, who in the Elvish tongue is named Ilúvatar, made the Ainur of his thought; and they made a great Music before him.' — The Silmarillion, Ainulindalë (1977)","legacySummary":"The Silmarillion contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Silmarillion is a posthumously published collection of mythopoeic writings by J.R.R. Tolkien (1892–1973), edited by his son Christopher Tolkien and published by George Allen & Unwin on 15 September 1977 — four years after the author's death. The book presents the history of Middle-earth's earliest ages in five interconnected sections: the Ainulindalë (the creation myth — Ilúvatar creates the world through the 'Music of the Ainur'); the Valaquenta (an account of the angelic Valar and Maiar); the Quenta Silmarillion (the vast history of the First Age, centring on three jewels — the Silmarils, created by the Noldorin elf Fëanor — containing the light of the Two Trees of Valinor and stolen by Morgoth, the first Dark Lord); the Akallabêth (the downfall of the island kingdom of Númenor); and Of the Rings of Power and the Third Age (a summary bridging to The Lord of the Rings).

The Silmarillion reveals The Lord of the Rings as the visible surface of a mythological system of extraordinary depth and internal consistency — a legendarium on which Tolkien worked from approximately 1916 (the 'Book of Lost Tales,' begun in a hospital tent during the Battle of the Somme) to his death in 1973. The book draws explicitly on Norse mythology (the Ainulindalë echoes Völuspá), Finnish mythology (the Kalevala was Tolkien's direct model for tone and structure), Old English epic (Beowulf), Celtic mythology, and Christian theology (the Fall of the Noldor parallels the Fall of Man, and Morgoth is explicitly a Satanic figure). Tolkien regarded the creation of a modern mythology — to supply what he considered England's lack of a native mythological tradition comparable to the Norse or Finnish — as a central project of his literary life.

The Silmarillion transformed the understanding of Tolkien's achievement and established secondary world-building as the foundational ambition of modern fantasy literature. The creation of fully realised fictional worlds with their own languages, histories, mythologies, and geographies — demonstrated by Middle-earth's extraordinary depth — has been the defining aspiration of fantasy writers from Ursula K. Le Guin and George R.R. Martin to Brandon Sanderson. The book also demonstrated that the 20th century was capable of producing mythology with genuine spiritual depth; its influence on popular culture, accelerated by Peter Jackson's Lord of the Rings films (2001–2003) and Amazon's The Rings of Power series (2022–), continues to expand.","historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Silmarillion has had a lasting impact on modern fantasy literature, influencing countless authors and creators.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
